%[text]{"align":"center"} # FOC Default Controller Gains
%[text] The [FOC Default Controller Gains](https://www.mathworks.com/help/mcb/ref/focdefaultcontrollergains.html) is a block from the [Motor Control Blockset library](https://www.mathworks.com/help/mcb/index.html). This block calculates controller gains for PI controllers used in field-oriented control (FOC) algorithms based on an empirical method or optimum theory. 
%[text] You'll be computing controller gains based on empirical method for CURRENT and SPEED control. Therefore current controller and speed controller must be checked in the block parameters.
%[text] Your block parameters and block should look like this: 
%[text] ![](text:image:841a)![](text:image:4fd6)
%[text] This script will help provide the inputs for the block for your PMSM BSM5ON-133 to calculate the controller gains for speed control. 
%[text] Rs is the stator resistance of the motor (in ohms). The value is provided by the motor datasheet. You can find it in the PMSM Block Parameters under Main, "Stator Resistance per phase, Rs".
%[text] ![](text:image:1af3)
Rs = 0.3;
%[text] Ld is the d-axis inductance of the motor (in henries). The value is provided by the motor datasheet. You can find it in the PMSM Block Parameters under Main, "Stator d-axis inductance, Ld". 
%[text] ![](text:image:0baa)
Ld = 0.0018;
%[text] Lq is the q-axis inductance of the motor (in henries). The value is provided by the motor datasheet. You can find it in the PMSM Block Parameters under Main, "Stator q-axis inductance, Lq".
%[text] ![](text:image:3a49)
Lq = 0.0018;
%[text] J is the inertia of the motor (in kg.m2). The value is provided by the motor datasheet. You can find it in the PMSM Block Parameters under Mechanical, "Rotor Inertia".
%[text] ![](text:image:07a8)
J = 0.0243;
%[text] Ts is the sample time of the current PI controller used in the FOC based motor control algorithm (in seconds). We will be using the sample time of the solver configuration block for implementing FOC. The new solver configuration block sample time will be 5e-5 for better time resolution. 
Ts = 5e-5;
%[text] Tsc is the sample time of the speed PI controller used in the FOC based motor control algorithm (in seconds). We will be using the sample time of the solver configuration block for implementing FOC. 
Tsc = 5e-5;
%[text] SlowdownFactor is a factor between 1 and 20 that can be provided to modify the output PI gains. A higher value makes the controller parameters less aggressive and slows down the system response. You will be using a value of 1 for now. 
SF = 1;
%[text] Imax is the peak value of the maximum AC current magnitude in an inverter-based motor drive system (in amperes). The value is the Rated current (A), which you can find in the PMSM Block Parameterization Manager (as seen before). 
IMax = 10.2;
%%
%[text] ## Sending Variables to the Model Workspace
%[text] Make sure to have your Simulink Model open to be able to send the variables needed for your simulation to your Model Workspace. 
%[text] Remember, variables in the Model Workspace take priority over variables in the MATLAB Workspace, so make sure to update your variables when needed, either using a similar script or by entering the Model Workspace in Simulink. 
mw8 = Simulink.data.connect("SMXQ6_600_FOCgains.slx");
set(mw8,"Rs",Rs);
set(mw8,"Ld",Ld);
set(mw8,"Lq",Lq);
set(mw8, "J", J);
set(mw8, "Ts", Ts);
set(mw8, "Tsc", Tsc);
set(mw8, "SF", SF);
set(mw8, "IMax", IMax);
%%
%[text] ## After Running your Model - Saving your Output Parameters
%[text] Log the output parameters that you intend to save:
%[text] ![](text:image:415d)
%[text] Run the model with a stop time of 0s, and run this section of the script to see your parameters in a table. 
gains = out.logsout.extractTimetable %[output:477e0c6c]
%[text] Now, we can extract your parameters and save them for the next guided lesson. 
DaxisKi = gains.("<Daxis_Ki>");
DaxisKp = gains.("<Daxis_Kp>");
QaxisKi = gains.("<Qaxis_Ki>");
QaxisKp = gains.("<Qaxis_Kp>");
SpeedKi = gains.("<Speed_Ki>");
SpeedKp = gains.("<Speed_Kp>");
save("FOCgains.mat", "DaxisKi","DaxisKp","QaxisKi","QaxisKp","SpeedKi","SpeedKp")
%%
